          tokens / authorization codes in implicit or hybrid flows (token/code substitution).               
                                                                                                            
           ▶▶┆ Fixed for authlib at version: 1.6.9
          325┆ [[package]]
   
   ❯❯❱ authlib - CVE-2026-28490
          Severity: HIGH                                                                                   
          Affected versions of authlib are vulnerable to Observable Discrepancy / Use of a Broken or Risky 
          Cryptographic Algorithm. Authlib's JOSE JWE implementation enables a Bleichenbacher-style padding
          oracle when decrypting JWEs using the `RSA1_5` key management algorithm: the `unwrap()` code     
          performs a post-decryption CEK length check and raises a distinct `ValueError('Invalid "cek"     
          length')` for invalid PKCS#1 v1.5 padding, while valid padding proceeds to AES-GCM and fails with
          `InvalidTag`. Because `RSA1_5` is available by default and these error conditions are externally 
          distinguishable (e.g., via HTTP responses/logs in common Python web frameworks), an attacker can 
          adaptively query a JWE decryption endpoint to recover the CEK and decrypt or forge JWE payloads  
          without the RSA private key.                                                                     
                                                                                                           
           ▶▶┆ Fixed for authlib at version: 1.6.9
          325┆ [[package]]
   
   ❯❯❱ authlib - CVE-2025-61920
          Severity: HIGH                                                                                     
          Affected versions of authlib are vulnerable to Allocation of Resources Without Limits or Throttling
          / Improper Input Validation / Uncontrolled Resource Consumption. Authlib's JOSE implementation does
          not enforce size limits on JWS/JWT header or signature segments. A remote attacker can submit a    
          token whose base64url-encoded header or signature spans hundreds of megabytes, forcing Authlib to  
          decode and parse the entire payload before rejecting it. This drives CPU and memory consumption to 
          hostile levels, enabling a denial-of-service against any service that verifies such tokens.        
                                                                                                             
           ▶▶┆ Fixed for authlib at version: 1.6.5
          325┆ [[package]]
   
   ❯❯❯❱ authlib - CVE-2026-27962
          Severity: CRITICAL                                                                                  
          Affected versions of authlib are vulnerable to Improper Verification of Cryptographic Signature.    
          Authlib's JWS implementation is vulnerable to JWK Header Injection: when `key=None` is passed to any
          JWS deserialization function (or when a key resolver callable returns `None` for an unknown `kid`), 
          the library silently extracts and trusts the attacker-embedded `jwk` header field as the            
          verification key, allowing an unauthenticated attacker to forge arbitrary JWT tokens with any claims
          (e.g., admin roles, elevated scopes) that pass signature verification — bypassing authentication and
          authorization entirely.                                                                             
                                                                                                              
           ▶▶┆ Fixed for authlib at version: 1.6.9
          325┆ [[package]]
   
   ❯❯❱ cryptography - CVE-2026-26007
          Severity: HIGH                                                                                     
          Affected versions of cryptography are vulnerable to Insufficient Verification of Data Authenticity.
          Missing subgroup validation in cryptography's SECT (binary) curve public‐key APIs: the functions   
          public_key_from_numbers, EllipticCurvePublicNumbers.public_key(), load_der_public_key(), and       
          load_pem_public_key() do not check that supplied points lie in the curve's prime‐order subgroup. An
          attacker can submit a small‐order subgroup point to leak bits of the victim's private key via ECDH 
